This study examines the secular evolution of spinning binaries, incorporating quadrupole effects, for systems comprising either gravastars or boson stars alongside neutron stars or black holes. We demonstrate that the secular precession of the binary's orbital plane is governed by the combined precession frequencies of the individual bodies. When these frequencies differ, the plane oscillates; if they match, the plane remains fixed in space. We further analyze the specific conditions required for these frequencies to coincide, and we explore the implications of these findings for the orbital dynamics of such binaries.

